---
// ============================================================
// /about/ — who Anupam is, and what the site is for.
//
// The biography comes first, then the material about the site
// that used to live on the home page.
//
// THE PHOTO. It is imported from src/assets/ rather than sitting
// in public/, and that difference matters. A file in public/ is
// served exactly as it is, at full size. A file imported like
// this goes through Astro's optimiser, which resizes it, makes a
// sharper copy for high-density screens, and writes the width and
// height into the markup so the page does not jump as it loads.
// ============================================================

import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageShell from "../components/PageShell.astro";
import SideBlock from "../components/SideBlock.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import profilePhoto from "../assets/profile-photo.webp";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Anupam Kumar and this site: writing, cleaned datasets and browser-side tools on public finance and public policy."
>
  <PageShell asideLabel="Elsewhere">
    <article data-pagefind-body>
      <h1>About</h1>

      <div class="profile">
        <!-- The panel behind the photo is not decoration. The image
             has a transparent background and the suit is black, so
             on the dark theme the silhouette would dissolve into the
             page. The sunk surface is a step lighter than the page
             in dark and a step darker in light, which gives the
             outline something to sit against in both.

             loading="eager" because it is at the top of the page;
             lazy-loading something already in view only delays it. -->
        <div class="profile-photo">
          <Image
            src={profilePhoto}
            alt="Anupam Kumar"
            width={192}
            densities={[1, 2]}
            loading="eager"
          />
        </div>

        <div class="profile-text prose">
          <h2>Anupam Kumar</h2>

          <p>
            I am a Program Associate on the Strategic Public Finance team at
            the{" "}
            <a href="https://cegis.org">
              Centre for Effective Governance of Indian States
            </a>{" "}
            (CEGIS), where I work with state governments on how public money is
            raised, allocated and spent. Before joining CEGIS full time I
            interned with Absolute Reports, CHRIST Consulting and CEGIS itself —
            research, project management and a stretch in the education sector,
            which is where a good deal of what I write here comes from.
          </p>

          <p>
            I hold an MSc in Economics and Analytics from CHRIST (Deemed to be
            University), Pune–Lavasa, and an MBA from Quantic School of Business
            and Technology, Washington, D.C., which I attended on a full
            scholarship. My first degree is a BCom Honours from CHRIST (Deemed
            to be University), Bangalore, where I held consecutive scholarships
            for leadership.
          </p>

          <p>
            What holds my attention is the point where data-driven methods meet
            the social sector — not the technology for its own sake, but the
            narrower question of whether better measurement changes what a
            government actually does. Away from work I read, write, and am
            always glad of a good argument about any of the above.
          </p>

          <p>
            I am on{" "}
            <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/anupamkumar-connect/">LinkedIn</a>,
            and you can write to me at{" "}
            <a href="mailto:hello@anupamkumar.org">hello@anupamkumar.org</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What this site is for</h2>

        <p>
        Its purpose is narrow and deliberate: <strong
          >to make economic information usable by people who are not economists</strong
        >. Public finance in India is not short of numbers. It is short of
        numbers anyone can find, check and reuse.
        </p>

        <p>
        So the site holds three things, in the order they matter. Writing, which
        is the point. Cleaned datasets, each carrying its source, the date it
        was retrieved, a description of every column with its unit, and a script
        that rebuilds it from the original files. And small tools that run
        entirely in your browser.
        </p>

        <h2>On the data</h2>

        <p>
        Anyone can post a spreadsheet. What makes a dataset worth citing is that
        it answers the four questions a careful reader always has: where exactly
        did this come from, what vintage is it, what are the units, and what was
        changed?
        </p>

        <p>
        Every dataset here answers all four. The original files are kept
        untouched alongside the cleaned ones, and the cleaning can be re-run
        from scratch by anyone who wants to check it.
        </p>

        <p>
        One thing in particular gets stated rather than assumed: Budget
        Estimates, Revised Estimates and Actuals for the same year are three
        different facts, not one figure corrected twice. Conflating them is the
        most common error in Indian fiscal datasets, and it quietly corrupts
        every comparison built on top.
        </p>

        <h2>On the tools</h2>

        <p>
        Everything computes in your browser. If you open a state finance
        spreadsheet in a tool here, that file never leaves your laptop, because
        there is no server for it to go to. For anyone in government, a think
        tank or a newsroom handling figures that are not yet public, that is not
        a technical detail.
        </p>

        <h2>On how this site is built</h2>

        <p>
        It is built to outlive its hosting. Everything of value — every essay,
        every dataset, every script — is a plain text file in{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/asrshadow/anupamkumar.org">a public repository</a>,
        readable in any text editor, with no database and no service required to
        make sense of it.
        </p>

        <p>
        The hosting is assumed to be temporary and the domain is assumed to be
        permanent. If this site has to be rebuilt somewhere else in 2035, that
        is a clone and a build, not a recovery project.
        </p>

        <p>
        Nothing here tracks you. There is no analytics script, no cookie banner
        and nothing to consent to, because there is no server collecting
        anything in the first place.
        </p>

        <h2>Reuse and citation</h2>

        <p>
        The writing and the datasets are published under{" "}
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>, and
        the code under the MIT licence. Please reuse any of it, with credit.
        </p>

        <p>
        Official figures republished here keep their own publisher's licence,
        which is recorded with each dataset.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>

        <p>
          Corrections are especially welcome. On a site whose whole claim is
          provenance, a correction is a contribution rather than a complaint.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <Fragment slot="aside">
      <SideBlock title="Elsewhere">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/anupamkumar-connect/">LinkedIn</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://asranupam.substack.com">Newsletter on Substack</a>
          </li>
          <li><a href="mailto:hello@anupamkumar.org">hello@anupamkumar.org</a></li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://github.com/asrshadow/anupamkumar.org">Source on GitHub</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </SideBlock>

      <SideBlock title="This site">
        <ul class="section-links">
          <li>
            <a href="/writing/">Writings and notes</a>
            <span>Essays, notes and archived newsletter pieces.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/datasets/">Datasets</a>
            <span>Cleaned public finance data, with its provenance.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/tools/">Tools</a>
            <span>Small things that run in your browser.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </SideBlock>
    </Fragment>
  </PageShell>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Photo beside the biography on a wide screen, above it on a
     phone. auto-fit with a minimum does that without a media
     query, and never squeezes the text into a narrow ribbon. */
  .profile {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(16rem, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-6);
    align-items: start;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-7);
  }

  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .profile {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  /* See the note in the markup: this panel is what keeps a
     transparent-backed portrait in a dark suit from dissolving
     into the page on the dark theme. */
  .profile-photo {
    padding: var(--space-4);
    background: var(--colour-surface-sunk);
    border-radius: var(--radius-data);
    line-height: 0; /* no phantom gap under the image */

    /* Hug the photo instead of stretching. A grid item fills its
       column by default, which on a phone left the panel running
       the full width of the screen with the photo marooned in the
       middle of it. */
    justify-self: start;
  }

  .profile-photo :global(img) {
    border-radius: 0;
    margin: 0;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* The first heading in the column should sit level with the top
     of the photo rather than pushing away from it. */
  .profile-text > :first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* Each section link with a line saying what is there, so the
     block is useful rather than a bare list of words. */
  .section-links li {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-1);
  }

  .section-links span {
    color: var(--colour-ink-muted);
  }
</style>
